ALLOY IQ — full refined problem statement
The product in one sentence
A SaaS platform that predicts, interprets, and optimizes mechanical and corrosion properties of steels, high-entropy alloys, and aluminum alloys from composition and processing parameters — with explainability that a metallurgist trusts and a 3D microstructure visualization that no competing tool offers.

The commercial problem
Material qualification is the longest lead-time item in any product development cycle. A new aerospace alloy takes 10–15 years from discovery to certification. An automotive OEM switching a structural steel needs 18–36 months of physical testing just to validate a candidate. The tools available today fall into two categories:
CALPHAD software (Thermo-Calc, Pandat) — thermodynamically rigorous but requires trained specialists, costs $15,000+/seat/year, and predicts equilibrium phase diagrams, not the final mechanical properties of a processed component.
Internal databases — every major materials supplier (Carpenter, Arconic, Novelis) has one, but it's locked behind NDAs and only covers their own alloy portfolio.
MetallurgIQ sits between these: accessible to any engineer with a browser, trained on public data, and outputting the properties that actually go into a design specification — not just phase fractions.

Target verticals and their specific pain points
Aerospace and defense engineers are qualifying new Al alloys for lightweighting and HEAs for high-temperature turbine applications. They need fatigue limit and fracture toughness data before committing to physical coupon testing campaigns. One avoided test campaign at $200k+ is a year of SaaS revenue.
Oil and gas materials engineers are specifying stainless and duplex steels for sour service environments. The corrosion module — specifically PREN prediction and pitting potential estimation — maps directly to their procurement specs (NACE MR0175 compliance).
Additive manufacturing material suppliers are characterizing HEA and steel powders where composition varies batch-to-batch. The inverse design engine lets them answer "what's the closest in-spec composition if my Cr content came in 0.3% low?"
Defense and government labs (the R&D buyer) need interpretable models for materials qualification reports — SHAP waterfall charts export directly into technical deliverables.

Technical problem statement (the honest version)
The central technical challenge is heterogeneous data: four properties across three alloy families gives twelve data availability situations ranging from excellent (steel hardness, ~8,000 labeled points) to genuinely sparse (HEA corrosion, ~150 usable entries). The platform's intellectual contribution is a principled strategy for each cell, not a single monolithic model.
For well-covered cells (steel YS, steel hardness, Al YS): standard stacking ensemble — XGBoost + Random Forest + MLP — with Magpie elemental descriptors and Bayesian hyperparameter optimization via Optuna. These are the cells where R² > 0.93 is achievable and where the SHAP analysis produces commercially credible narratives.
For moderately covered cells (HEA YS/hardness, steel fatigue/corrosion, Al hardness/fatigue): physics-informed feature engineering fills the gap. For steels: carbon equivalent, PREN, prior austenite grain size proxy. For HEAs: ΔSmix (configurational entropy), VEC (valence electron concentration), δ (atomic size mismatch), and ΔHmix from Miedema's model. These descriptors are computed from composition alone and inject thermodynamic grounding that pure Magpie features miss.
For sparse cells (HEA fatigue, HEA corrosion, Al corrosion): transfer learning from data-rich families plus uncertainty-quantified predictions displayed with wide confidence intervals. The product is transparent about this — a "data confidence" badge on every prediction tells the user how much to trust it. Honesty about uncertainty is itself a competitive differentiator vs tools that output a number with no context.

The four differentiating features
The SHAP narrative layer. Every prediction generates a waterfall chart decomposed by element and processing parameter. The system generates a plain-English interpretation: "Molybdenum is the single largest driver of corrosion resistance in this composition, contributing +12 PREN units. Sulfur is your largest risk factor at -3.2 PREN." This is the feature that gets a demo past a PhD materials scientist.
The inverse design engine. Given a target property profile (e.g. YS > 900 MPa, KIc > 80 MPa√m, PREN > 35), a multi-objective genetic algorithm using DEAP searches the composition space under element-fraction constraints. The result is a Pareto front of candidate alloys — a 2D scatter plot where every point is a real candidate, and the curve shows the fundamental trade-off between the objectives. No competing tool does this interactively.
Uncertainty quantification with conformal prediction. Every output shows a calibrated prediction interval — not just "YS = 850 MPa" but "YS = 850 ± 65 MPa at 90% confidence." Conformal prediction guarantees this interval's coverage without distributional assumptions. This is what makes the output defensible in an engineering report.
The Blender microstructure visualizer. Predicted phase fractions (martensite %, ferrite %, carbide precipitates, austenite retention) feed a Python script running in Blender's scripting environment. Voronoi tessellation generates grain boundaries, particle systems place carbide precipitates at predicted volume fractions, and volume shaders color phases by their crystal structure. The output is a render that gives a materials scientist an immediate visual check on whether the prediction is physically plausible. No other materials informatics product does this..